  // design: Tileset 11, step E1 (docs/map-briefs/urban/URBAN_PLAN.md). One cell, one level: eight steps (0.25 m by 0.4 m) east up the outer lane (z -1.2 to 1.2) to a half landing at 2 m on the east end, eight back west up the lane by the wall to a landing at 4 m on the west end (x -4 to -1.6), where the next one up starts (its own cell is the level above, so they stack; the top landing and its rail stand in that level and overlap none of its parts). Lanes are 2.4 m wide (the one by the wall 2.8 m, as a wall stands 0.25 m into the cell) and the landings 2.4 m long: bots need it (the navmesh keeps 0.8 m from every edge and drops a strip much under 0.6 m; at 2 m lanes, and with 2 m landings whose middle post closed the turn, they did not path it; for the same reason the rail between the lanes stops 0.65 m short of the top landing, where the next stair's foot is). A straight flight to a balcony along the whole wall was tried and does not fit a cell: sixteen steps need 6.4 m (the navmesh does not take 0.3 m steps), leaving 0.8 m landings. So the storey's landing is the west 2.4 m of the wall: a centred Door (3 m) misses it; an Arch, a Wide doorway's west side or an open side reaches it, and the twin windows look onto it. Treads are 0.3 m thick, each bearing on the next; rails are two thin bars on posts, so shots pass; the ground-level start has no rail, so it is walked onto from the west or north. The wall side has no rail: it is meant against a building.
